 ((98
  ("91" 0.8538 0.8729 0.0728 0.0862 0.0862 0.0134 nil nil 0.0191)
  ("That is, the procedure modiﬁes the tableau in such a way that the disjunction of" 0.1812 0.8730 0.1243 0.1377 0.1377 0.0134 0.0073 0.2313 0.0414)
  ("the formulas represented by the leaf nodes of the resulting tableau is equivalent to" 0.1812 0.8730 0.1439 0.1573 0.1573 0.0134 0.0065 0.2297 0.0266)
  ("the original one. One of these conjunctions may contain a pair of complementary" 0.1812 0.8730 0.1635 0.1769 0.1769 0.0134 0.0072 0.2651 0.0266)
  ("literals, in which case that conjunction is equivalent to false. If all conjunctions are" 0.1812 0.8729 0.1831 0.1965 0.1965 0.0134 0.0059 0.2541 0.0617)
  ("equivalent to false, the original formula is unsatisﬁable. This shows the soundness" 0.1812 0.8729 0.2028 0.2162 0.2162 0.0134 0.0068 0.2582 0.0845)
  ("of the procedure." 0.1812 0.3236 0.2224 0.2358 0.2358 0.0134 0.0064 0.1873 0.0154)
  ("If a formula is unsatisﬁable, then every term in its DNF must be equivalent" 0.2282 0.8730 0.2420 0.2554 0.2554 0.0134 0.0072 0.2861 0.0127)
  ("to false and the corresponding node in the tableau is closed. Thus we will have a" 0.1812 0.8730 0.2616 0.2750 0.2750 0.0134 0.0071 0.2353 0.0170)
  ("closed tableau. This shows the completeness of the procedure." 0.1812 0.7020 0.2812 0.2947 0.2947 0.0134 0.0064 0.2496 0.0501)
  ("Since neither α-rules nor β-rules can be used forever (each rule reduces ei-" 0.2282 0.8729 0.3009 0.3143 0.3143 0.0134 0.0082 0.1894 0.0436)
  ("ther one occurrence of a binary operator or two negations), the procedure must be" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3205 0.3339 0.3339 0.0134 0.0064 0.2195 0.0340)
  ("terminating." 0.1812 0.2843 0.3401 0.3535 0.3535 0.0134 nil 0.3195 0.1031)
  ("2" 0.8583 0.8729 0.3363 0.3559 0.3559 0.0196 nil nil 0.0146)
  ("Of course, semantic tableau can be used to show the satisﬁability of a formula." 0.2282 0.8730 0.3688 0.3822 0.3822 0.0134 0.0051 0.3114 0.0207)
  ("If there is a model node in the tableau, we may assign 1 to every literal in this node" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3885 0.4019 0.4019 0.0134 0.0056 0.2967 0.0127)
  ("so that the formula represented by this node is true under this interpretation. The" 0.1812 0.8730 0.4081 0.4215 0.4215 0.0134 0.0063 0.2397 0.0171)
  ("original formula will be true under this interpretation because it is equivalent to the" 0.1812 0.8730 0.4277 0.4411 0.4411 0.0134 0.0054 0.2828 0.0627)
  ("disjunction of the formulas of the leaf nodes. Thus, this interpretation is a model" 0.1812 0.8729 0.4473 0.4607 0.4607 0.0134 0.0071 0.2624 0.0920)
  ("of the original formula. This shows that the procedure can be used to ﬁnd a model" 0.1812 0.8730 0.4669 0.4804 0.4804 0.0134 0.0061 0.2705 0.0154)
  ("when the formula is satisﬁable." 0.1812 0.4408 0.4866 0.5000 0.5000 0.0134 0.0064 0.2889 0.0436)
  ("Semantic tableau speciﬁes what rules to be used but it does not specify which" 0.2282 0.8730 0.5062 0.5196 0.5196 0.0134 0.0056 0.2760 0.0760)
  ("rule should be used ﬁrst or which node should be expanded ﬁrst. In general, α-rules" 0.1812 0.8729 0.5258 0.5392 0.5392 0.0134 0.0054 0.2625 0.0319)
  ("are better be applied before β-rules, so that the number of nodes in a tree can be" 0.1812 0.8729 0.5454 0.5588 0.5588 0.0134 0.0069 0.2216 0.0255)
  ("reduced. In practice, the order of formulas in a list can be changed and the order" 0.1812 0.8730 0.5651 0.5785 0.5785 0.0134 0.0070 0.2203 0.0701)
  ("of β1 and β2 can be switched. This gives the user the freedom to construct trees of" 0.1812 0.8730 0.5847 0.5994 0.5981 0.0134 0.0062 0.2439 0.0154)
  ("diﬀerent shapes, or to ﬁnd a model node quicker. The procedure may terminate once" 0.1812 0.8730 0.6043 0.6177 0.6177 0.0134 0.0046 0.3080 0.0691)
  ("a model node is found, if the goal is to show that the original formula is satisﬁable." 0.1812 0.8729 0.6239 0.6373 0.6373 0.0134 0.0060 0.2916 0.0096)
  ("Semantic tableaux are much more expressive and easy to use than truth-tables," 0.2282 0.8730 0.6436 0.6570 0.6570 0.0134 0.0045 0.3007 0.0760)
  ("though that is not the reason for their introduction. Sometimes, a tableau uses more" 0.1812 0.8730 0.6632 0.6766 0.6766 0.0134 0.0048 0.3040 0.0584)
  ("space than a truth table. For example, an unsatisﬁable full CNF of n variables will" 0.1812 0.8729 0.6828 0.6962 0.6962 0.0134 0.0060 0.2903 0.0447)
  ("generate a tableau of more than n! nodes, which is large than a truth table of 2n" 0.1812 0.8721 0.7004 0.7158 0.7158 0.0134 0.0073 0.2257 0.0701)
  ("lines. The beauty of semantic tableaux lies on the simplicity of presenting a proof" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7221 0.7355 0.7355 0.0134 0.0068 0.2676 0.0426)
  ("procedure using a set of α and β rules. We will see in later chapters how new" 0.1812 0.8729 0.7417 0.7551 0.7551 0.0134 0.0087 0.2493 0.0824)
  ("rules are added into this procedure so that a proof procedure for other logics can" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7613 0.7747 0.7747 0.0134 0.0073 0.1821 0.0394)
  ("be obtained." 0.1812 0.2848 0.7809 0.7943 0.7943 0.0134 0.0064 0.2042 0.0197))
  (99
  ("92" 0.8538 0.8729 0.0728 0.0862 0.0862 0.0134 nil nil 0.0191)
  ("3.2" 0.1812 0.2093 0.1293 0.1427 0.1427 0.0134 nil 0.2858 0.0281)
  ("Deductive Systems" 0.2312 0.4133 0.1293 0.1427 0.1427 0.0134 0.0073 0.2838 0.0967)
  ("In logic, a deductive system S consists of a set of inference rules, where each" 0.2282 0.8730 0.1463 0.1659 0.1636 0.0134 0.0066 0.2436 0.0175)
  ("rule takes premises as input and returns a conclusion (or conclusions) as output." 0.1812 0.8729 0.1698 0.1832 0.1832 0.0134 0.0078 0.2055 0.0319)
  ("Popular inference rules in propositional logic include modus ponens (MP), modus" 0.1812 0.8730 0.1894 0.2028 0.2028 0.0134 0.0072 0.2848 0.0656)
  ("tollens (MT), and contraposition (CP), which can be displayed, respectively, as" 0.1812 0.8730 0.2090 0.2224 0.2224 0.0134 0.0090 0.2623 0.0543)
  ("follows:" 0.1812 0.2429 0.2287 0.2421 0.2421 0.0134 nil 0.3527 0.0617)
  ("A→B" 0.2488 0.3080 0.2711 0.2907 0.2884 0.0196 nil 0.1162 0.0593)
  ("B" 0.2911 0.3056 0.2955 0.3089 0.3089 0.0134 nil nil 0.0145)
  ("A" 0.3345 0.3489 0.2750 0.2884 0.2884 0.0134 nil nil 0.0143)
  ("(MP)" 0.3636 0.4090 0.2852 0.2986 0.2986 0.0134 nil 0.3259 0.0454)
  ("A → B ¬B" 0.4492 0.5625 0.2711 0.2907 0.2907 0.0196 0.0054 0.4902 0.0143)
  ("(MT)" 0.5782 0.6244 0.2852 0.2986 0.2986 0.0134 nil 0.3223 0.0462)
  ("¬A" 0.4926 0.5200 0.2917 0.3113 0.3113 0.0196 nil nil 0.0274)
  ("A→B" 0.6776 0.7369 0.2711 0.2907 0.2884 0.0196 nil 0.1161 0.0593)
  ("(CP)" 0.7656 0.8073 0.2852 0.2986 0.2986 0.0134 nil 0.2482 0.0417)
  ("¬B → ¬A" 0.6646 0.7509 0.2917 0.3113 0.3113 0.0196 0.0064 0.4028 0.0276)
  ("3.2.1 Inference Rules and Proofs" 0.1812 0.4646 0.3563 0.3697 0.3697 0.0134 0.0064 0.3217 0.0393)
  ("In general, an inference rule can be speciﬁed as" 0.2282 0.6237 0.3772 0.3906 0.3906 0.0134 0.0064 0.2225 0.0175)
  ("P1" 0.4533 0.4726 0.4068 0.4215 0.4215 0.0134 nil nil 0.0192)
  ("···" 0.5349 0.5577 0.4029 0.4225 0.4225 0.0196 nil 0.0011 0.0228)
  ("P2" 0.4925 0.5117 0.4068 0.4215 0.4215 0.0134 nil nil 0.0192)
  ("Pk" 0.5801 0.5996 0.4068 0.4215 0.4215 0.0134 nil nil 0.0195)
  ("C" 0.5195 0.5332 0.4273 0.4407 0.4407 0.0134 nil nil 0.0137)
  ("where P1 , P2 , ..., Pk are the premises and C is the conclusion. We may also write it" 0.1812 0.8729 0.4542 0.4689 0.4676 0.0134 0.0064 0.3756 0.0489)
  ("as" 0.1812 0.1983 0.4738 0.4872 0.4872 0.0134 nil nil 0.0171)
  ("P1 , P 2 , · · · , P k ` C" 0.4502 0.6025 0.4895 0.5092 0.5081 0.0196 0.0032 0.6453 0.0192)
  ("If the premises are empty (k = 0), we say this inference rule is an axiom rule." 0.1812 0.8306 0.5212 0.5346 0.5346 0.0134 0.0064 0.2957 0.0127)
  ("An inference rule is sound if its premises entail the conclusion, that is," 0.2282 0.8170 0.5408 0.5542 0.5542 0.0134 0.0064 0.2698 0.0250)
  ("P1 , P2 , · · · , Pk |= C" 0.4477 0.6051 0.5717 0.5913 0.5902 0.0196 0.0033 0.6022 0.0192)
  ("for every inference rule {P1 , P2 , · · · , Pk } ` C (or equivalently, (P1 ∧P2 ∧· · ·∧Pk ) → C" 0.1812 0.8715 0.6064 0.6260 0.6237 0.0196 0.0033 0.4654 0.0228)
  ("is a tautology). For example, MP (modus ponens) is sound because {A → B, A} |=" 0.1812 0.8729 0.6260 0.6457 0.6433 0.0134 0.0058 0.3635 0.0129)
  ("B; modus tollens is sound because {A → B, ¬B} |= ¬A." 0.1812 0.6531 0.6457 0.6653 0.6629 0.0196 0.0064 0.4019 0.0208)
  ("Given a set A of axioms, which are the formulas assumed to be true, a proof" 0.2282 0.8730 0.6691 0.6825 0.6825 0.0134 0.0067 0.2893 0.0490)
  ("of formula B in S is a sequence of formulas F1 , F2 , ..., Fn such that Fn = B and each" 0.1812 0.8730 0.6849 0.7045 0.7022 0.0134 0.0053 0.3845 0.0154)
  ("Fi is either a formula in A or can be generated by an inference rule of S, using the" 0.1812 0.8729 0.7045 0.7241 0.7218 0.0134 0.0063 0.2703 0.0171)
  ("formulas before Fi in the sequence as the premises. If such a proof exits, we denote" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7280 0.7427 0.7414 0.0134 0.0060 0.2735 0.0713)
  ("it by A `S B; the subscript S can be dropped if S is understood from the context." 0.1812 0.8730 0.7438 0.7639 0.7610 0.0134 0.0063 0.2666 0.0128)
  ("The proof procedure P (A, B) based on S is simply to show A `S B. Obviously," 0.1812 0.8730 0.7634 0.7835 0.7807 0.0134 0.0078 0.3012 0.0329)
  ("this is a deduction-style proof procedure." 0.1812 0.5242 0.7869 0.8003 0.8003 0.0134 0.0064 0.2200 0.0309))
