  ("assumed" 0.5674 0.6377 0.5948 0.6082 0.6082 0.0134 nil 0.2905 0.0703)
  ("4. q → r" 0.4164 0.4957 0.6091 0.6288 0.6264 0.0196 0.0061 0.5164 0.0149)
  ("MP, 1, 2" 0.5674 0.6342 0.6130 0.6264 0.6264 0.0134 0.0033 0.6159 0.0359)
  ("5. ¬q" 0.4164 0.4690 0.6274 0.6470 0.6470 0.0196 0.0163 0.3688 0.0149)
  ("MP, 3, 4" 0.5674 0.6342 0.6312 0.6446 0.6446 0.0134 0.0033 0.6159 0.0359)
  ("Every proof can be represented by a directed graph where each node is a" 0.2282 0.8730 0.6566 0.6700 0.6700 0.0134 0.0090 0.1628 0.0486)
  ("formula in the proof and if a formula B is derived from A1 , A2 , ..., Ak by an inference" 0.1812 0.8729 0.6762 0.6909 0.6897 0.0134 0.0047 0.4027 0.0638)
  ("rule, then there are edges (Ai , B), 1 ≤ i ≤ k, in the graph. This proof graph must" 0.1812 0.8730 0.6920 0.7116 0.7093 0.0134 0.0066 0.3252 0.0372)
  ("be acyclic as we assume that no formula can be removed from a proof without" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7155 0.7289 0.7289 0.0134 0.0088 0.2428 0.0197)
  ("destroying the proof. Every topological sort of the nodes of this graph should give" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7351 0.7485 0.7485 0.0134 0.0066 0.2206 0.0862)
  ("us a linear proof. Since every acyclic direct graph (DAG) can be converted into" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7547 0.7681 0.7681 0.0134 0.0081 0.2256 0.0182)
  ("a tree (by duplicating some nodes) and still preserve the parent-child relation, we" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7744 0.7878 0.7878 0.0134 0.0071 0.2360 0.0096)
  ("may present a proof by a tree as well. The proof tree for the above proof example" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7940 0.8074 0.8074 0.0134 0.0067 0.2511 0.0351)
  ("is given in Figure 3.2.1. It is clear that all assumed formulas (axioms) do not have" 0.1812 0.8730 0.8136 0.8270 0.8270 0.0134 0.0064 0.2836 0.0129)
  ("the incoming edges. The ﬁnal formula in the proof does not have outgoing edges." 0.1812 0.8730 0.8332 0.8466 0.8466 0.0134 0.0071 0.2388 0.0266)
  ("All intermediate formulas have both incoming and outgoing edges." 0.1812 0.7382 0.8529 0.8663 0.8663 0.0134 0.0064 0.2864 0.0250))
