  ("(p ∧ (p → q)) → q is a tautology. By Theorem 3.2.2, the proposition holds." 0.1812 0.8082 0.3300 0.3496 0.3473 0.0134 0.0064 0.3487 0.0170)
  ("2" 0.8583 0.8729 0.3300 0.3496 0.3496 0.0196 nil nil 0.0146)
  ("Hilbert systems originally assumed that A = ∅ (or A = >), thus every derived" 0.2282 0.8729 0.3587 0.3783 0.3760 0.0134 0.0054 0.3417 0.0595)
  ("formula is a tautology in a proof. This restriction is relaxed today so that we may" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3822 0.3956 0.3956 0.0134 0.0065 0.2686 0.0638)
  ("take a set of formulas as axioms. The truth of the formulas becomes conditional on" 0.1812 0.8730 0.4018 0.4152 0.4152 0.0134 0.0058 0.2985 0.0351)
  ("the truth of the axioms." 0.1812 0.3821 0.4215 0.4349 0.4349 0.0134 0.0064 0.2699 0.0266)
