  ("96" 0.8538 0.8729 0.0728 0.0862 0.0862 0.0134 nil nil 0.0191)
  ("3.2.3 Natural Deduction" 0.1812 0.3961 0.1249 0.1383 0.1383 0.0134 0.0191 0.3554 0.0393)
  ("Both of semantic tableaux and Hilbert systems for constructing proofs have" 0.2282 0.8730 0.1458 0.1592 0.1592 0.0134 0.0074 0.2493 0.0412)
  ("their disadvantages. Hilbert’s systems are diﬃcult to construct proofs in; their" 0.1812 0.8729 0.1654 0.1788 0.1788 0.0134 0.0091 0.2678 0.0393)
  ("main uses are metalogical, the small number of rules making it easier to prove results" 0.1812 0.8730 0.1850 0.1984 0.1984 0.0134 0.0046 0.3487 0.0414)
  ("about logic. The tableau method on the other hand is easy to use mechanically but," 0.1812 0.8730 0.2046 0.2180 0.2180 0.0134 0.0053 0.2854 0.0483)
  ("because of the form of the connective rules and the fact that a tableau starts from" 0.1812 0.8730 0.2243 0.2377 0.2377 0.0134 0.0065 0.2214 0.0644)
  ("the negation of the formula to be proved, the proofs that result are not a natural" 0.1812 0.8730 0.2439 0.2573 0.2573 0.0134 0.0072 0.2058 0.0266)
  ("sequence of easily justiﬁable steps. Likewise, very few proofs in mathematics are" 0.1812 0.8730 0.2635 0.2769 0.2769 0.0134 0.0077 0.2642 0.0729)
  ("from axioms directly. Mathematicians in practice usually reason in a more ﬂexible" 0.1812 0.8730 0.2831 0.2965 0.2965 0.0134 0.0065 0.3112 0.0388)
  ("way." 0.1812 0.2173 0.3028 0.3162 0.3162 0.0134 nil 0.2087 0.0361)
  ("Natural deduction is a deductive system in which logical reasoning is expressed" 0.2282 0.8730 0.3224 0.3358 0.3358 0.0134 0.0046 0.3047 0.0643)
  ("by inference rules closely related to the “natural” way of reasoning. This contrasts" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3420 0.3554 0.3554 0.0134 0.0064 0.2279 0.0202)
  ("with Hilbert systems, which instead use axiom rules as much as possible to express" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3616 0.3750 0.3750 0.0134 0.0061 0.3019 0.0372)
  ("the logical laws of deductive reasoning. Natural deduction in its modern form" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3813 0.3947 0.3947 0.0134 0.0095 0.2650 0.0266)
  ("was independently proposed by the German mathematician Gerhard Gentzen in" 0.1812 0.8730 0.4009 0.4143 0.4143 0.0134 0.0085 0.2514 0.0304)
  ("1934. In Gentzen’s natural deductive system, a formula is represented by a set A" 0.1812 0.8729 0.4205 0.4339 0.4339 0.0134 0.0072 0.2418 0.0436)
  ("of formulas, A = {A1 , A2 , ..., An }, where the comma in A are understood as ∨. To" 0.1812 0.8729 0.4363 0.4559 0.4535 0.0134 0.0067 0.4055 0.0154)
  ("avoid the confusion, we will write A as" 0.1812 0.5055 0.4597 0.4731 0.4731 0.0134 0.0064 0.3110 0.0441)
  ("(A1 | A2 | · · · | An )." 0.4473 0.6068 0.4906 0.5102 0.5092 0.0196 0.0054 0.4958 0.0287)
  ("A is called a sequent by Gentzen. If n = 0, i.e., A = ( ), which is equivalent to ⊥" 0.1812 0.8729 0.5253 0.5449 0.5426 0.0134 0.0071 0.3017 0.0143)
  ("or (⊥). Since we assume that A is a set, duplicated formulas are removed and the" 0.1812 0.8729 0.5488 0.5622 0.5622 0.0134 0.0067 0.2828 0.0170)
  ("order of formulas in A is irrelevant." 0.1812 0.4768 0.5684 0.5818 0.5818 0.0134 0.0064 0.2909 0.0436)
  ("Deﬁnition 3.2.8. A natural deductive system G consists of the following three in-" 0.1812 0.8729 0.5968 0.6164 0.6140 0.0134 0.0070 0.2780 0.0952)
  ("ference rules, plus the following logical rules which guide the introduction and elim-" 0.1812 0.8729 0.6203 0.6337 0.6337 0.0134 0.0065 0.2604 0.0579)
  ("ination of logical operators in the sequents." 0.1812 0.5396 0.6399 0.6533 0.6533 0.0134 0.0068 0.2121 0.0591)
  ("name" 0.3654 0.4166 0.6740 0.6874 0.6874 0.0134 nil 0.2400 0.0512)
