  ("Proposition 3.2.10. The deductive system G is sound, i.e., if A `G B, then A |= B." 0.1812 0.8729 0.6367 0.6568 0.6540 0.0134 0.0053 0.3634 0.1114)
  ("Proof. It is easy to check that every inference rule of G is sound. In particular," 0.1812 0.8729 0.6689 0.6885 0.6862 0.0134 0.0083 0.2111 0.0513)
  ("the rules of operator introduction preserve the logical equivalence. In the rules of" 0.1812 0.8729 0.6924 0.7058 0.7058 0.0134 0.0072 0.2018 0.0266)
  ("operator elimination, some sequents generate two results. For example, (A ∧ B | α)" 0.1812 0.8729 0.7081 0.7278 0.7254 0.0134 0.0059 0.3203 0.0707)
  ("infers both (a) (A | α) and (b) (B | α). If we bind the two results together by ∧," 0.1812 0.8729 0.7278 0.7474 0.7450 0.0134 0.0072 0.2523 0.0453)
  ("these rules preserve the logical equivalence, too. On the other hand, the cut rule" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7512 0.7646 0.7646 0.0134 0.0075 0.2136 0.0426)
